\section{Details}{


Two quantities are computed, and they are not the same thing:
\itemize{
\item \strong{pregnancy-related} -- died while pregnant, during childbirth, or within
the postpartum window, \emph{whatever the cause}
\item \strong{maternal} -- as above but within 42 days, and excluding deaths due to
violence or an accident
}

\code{sib.maternal.death.date} is only computable when the data identify
accidental deaths, which means DHS phase 7 and later, or MICS6 and later.
Otherwise it is \code{NA} and only the pregnancy-related column is usable -- the
same limitation applies to DHS phases 2--6 and to MICS4/MICS5.

Siblings who did not die of the relevant cause get a death date of \code{-1}
rather than \code{NA}, so that they still contribute exposure. Both columns are
set to \code{NA} for male siblings.

\strong{Note for MICS users:} the tables published in MICS survey reports under
the heading "Maternal mortality", with a column labelled "Maternal Deaths",
do \emph{not} report maternal deaths as defined above. UNICEF's own tabulation
syntax counts \code{MM22 = 1 | MM23 = 1 | (MM24 = 1 & MM25 < 42)} and never reads
the violence (\code{MM26}) or accident (\code{MM27}) items at all, despite the
footnote in the reports saying those causes are excluded. So the published
column is a \strong{pregnancy-related} count on a 42-day window. To reproduce it,
use \code{sib.preg_related.death.date} with \code{preg.window = "42days"}. See the
vignette "Working with MICS sibling history data".
\subsection{Choosing \code{na.action}}{

\code{na.action} has \strong{no default for the MICS styles}, because it is a
substantive choice about the estimand rather than a coding detail, and it
should be made deliberately and reported.

In MICS, the number of days after the end of a pregnancy (\code{MM25}) is asked
only of sisters who died within two months (\code{MM24 = 1}). When that day count
is missing, \code{na.action} decides whether she falls inside the 42-day maternal
window. In three MICS6 surveys examined this affected 5 of 38 such deaths in
Iraq 2018 and none at all in Zimbabwe 2019 or Madagascar 2018, so the choice
is usually immaterial -- but not always, and it only ever moves the maternal
column, never the pregnancy-related one.

\strong{\code{na.action} no longer does anything under \code{style = "dhs"}.} It governed how a
missing \code{sib.time.delivery.death} (\code{mm12}) was treated, and \code{mm12} is no
longer consulted by either DHS column --- the reference implementation states
